Polymorphism in C# can be confusing when two variables refer to the same object but a call behaves differently through each variable. The missing piece is often the difference between the reference's compile-time type and the object's runtime type.

This article traces those calls directly. You will see why overriding a virtual member differs from hiding a member with new , why explicit interface implementations are called through a particular interface, and why static interface contracts need a separate explanation.

Example scope: The three code blocks are independent, complete console programs intended for .NET 10. They have not been compiled or executed. Every predicted result below is documentation-based reasoning, not captured execution output.

Two types participate in polymorphism in C#

A variable has a type available to the compiler. An object created at runtime also has its concrete type. Those can differ when a derived object is referenced through a base-class variable or an implementing object is referenced through an interface.

Microsoft's polymorphism guide explains both treating derived objects as base objects and selecting overrides of virtual members at runtime. These are related ideas, but they are not permission to call every concrete member through every reference.

Start by examining the expression to the left of the dot. Its compile-time type tells you what member surface the call is using. Then ask whether the selected member participates in virtual dispatch or another documented dispatch mechanism.

The object is not replaced when another variable refers to it. Microsoft's object-reference documentation describes multiple variables referring to the same class instance. Changing the reference type changes the view available at the call site, not the identity of the object.

Keeping this distinction visible prevents vague explanations such as “the newest method runs” or “the base variable always uses the base method.” Each can be wrong depending on the member involved.

This article focuses on ordinary instance calls with the declared types shown in the examples. It does not introduce dynamic , reflection, extension-method selection, or a performance comparison. Those have separate questions and would obscure the dispatch rules being illustrated.

Virtual and override describe one extension point

A virtual base member provides an extension point in a hierarchy. A derived class can override that member. The selected virtual member still belongs to the same chain of overriding behavior.

The following example uses message rendering rather than geometric relationships. A decorated renderer supplies a specialized rendering body. The program calls it through both the concrete and base reference.

using System;

namespace OverrideExample;

internal static class Program
{
private static void Main()
{
var concrete = new DecoratedRenderer();
MessageRenderer throughBase = concrete;

Console.WriteLine(concrete.Render());
Console.WriteLine(throughBase.Render());
Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(concrete, throughBase));
}
}

public class MessageRenderer
{
public virtual string Render()
{
return "message";
}
}

public sealed class DecoratedRenderer : MessageRenderer
{
public override string Render()
{
return $"[{base.Render()}]";
}
}

Reasoning from the documented virtual-dispatch rule, both rendering calls reach DecoratedRenderer.Render . The base-reference call does not force the base implementation merely because the variable is declared as MessageRenderer .

Inside the override, base.Render() explicitly invokes the base body. The decoration uses that result. The base body does not run automatically before or after every override; it runs here because the implementation asks for it.

Microsoft's override and new guidance documents both the override relationship and using base to reach base behavior. This distinction matters when reading an override that replaces, rather than extends, the base operation.

The snippet's contract is intentionally small: return a message string. It does not model an extensible UI framework or claim that every derived renderer respects a larger application contract. The example isolates which body is selected.

For an introduction to the hierarchy syntax itself, the existing inheritance examples in C# cover that foundation. Here, the important observation is that a base reference can still invoke a derived override.

